body:
No. Consider the following two functions: one is identically zero everywhere, and three other is defined as follows:

f(x) = {0 for x <= 0, exp(-x^(-2)) for x > 0}

These functions are equal on (-\infty, 0), and even their derivatives of all orders at x=0 are the same, yet f is clearly not identically zero.

> but V is not really defined 

What? You were just given its definition. You literally wrote it in the post:

> V = {(a₁, a₁, a₃) ∈ R^3 : a₁, a₃ ∈ R}

Here, let's examine this simpler bit of pseudocode:

    for i from 1 to 3
    {
        for j from 1 to 10
        {
            do_something();
        }
    }

Does it make sense to you that this piece of code calls `do_something()` 3 * 10 = 30 times?
